  "locked_narration": "After a lifetime of struggle and eventual success, Charles Bukowski left two words on his tombstone: 'Don't try.' He faced 30 years of rejection. His breakthrough at 50 seemed like an underdog tale. But success came from accepting who he was, not from wanting it more. This is 'The Backwards Law,' from Alan Watts. The harder you chase good feelings, the more they elude you. Straining for success makes you feel like a failure. Sometimes, the boldest moves only happen when we stop obsessing over the outcome. Accept discomfort, and it loses its power. What if you just let yourself feel it?",
